hi this is dr. Gregory Sadler I'm a  professor of philosophy and the  president and founder of an educational  consulting company called reason IO  where we put philosophy into practice  I've studied and taught philosophy for  over 20 years and I find that many  people run into difficulties reading  classic philosophical texts sometimes  it's the way things are said or how the  text is structured but the concepts  themselves are not always that  complicated and that's where I come in  to help students and lifelong learners  I've been producing longer lecture  videos and posting them to youtube many  viewers say they find them useful what  you're currently watching is part of a  new series of shorter videos each of  them focused on one core concept from an  important philosophical text I hope you  find it useful as well  at the beginning of fear and trembling  Kierkegaard is going to tell us four  little stories essentially  reinterpretations imagining what it must  have been like for Abraham on his way  and coming back from the commanded  sacrifice of his son Isaac why does he  begin a work of philosophy this way  well Abraham is really the central  figure and Abraham is being used here as  a way to try to understand faith and it  is being done from a philosophical  perspective that philosophy can use  narrative so that's part of what is is  going on here Abraham if you think about  his cultural significance is often  called the father of faith and we even  speak about the Abrahamic religions  referring primarily to Judaism  Christianity and Islam as the largest  branches of that entire family and why  do we talk about Abraham as the father  of faith this is part of what this book  is attempting to grapple with so we  begin with a narrative and you can find  this narrative in its original form in  the book of Genesis as a matter of fact  Abraham takes up quite a bit of space in  the book of Genesis and this this  incident of the sacrifice of Isaac takes  up an entire chapter so Abraham is a  person who is called by God he leaves  his ancestral home in Mesopotamia and  begins traveling following the the call  to go to the essentially Promised Land  where God tells him that he is going to  make him  [Music]  a mode or a vehicle of blessing for the  peoples of the earth so there's a  long-standing relationship developed  between Abraham and God and it includes  Abraham's family as well what ends up  happening is towards the end of their  their lives Abraham and Sarah don't have  any any children and this is kind of a  catastrophe not one that's easy for us  in the present day to relate to unless  we value childhood and birth and all of  the things that go along with that  generativity passing on life from  generation to generation but for ancient  peoples this was a terrible curse in  crisis  Sarah is barren well Abraham isn't  because he has a son with his handmaid  Hagar and this means that the promise  that God is making seems to be difficult  to fulfill how possibly can Abraham do  anything for the world once he's gone if  he's not going to have any progeny and  what ends up happening is there is a  miracle they have a child Isaac and  Abraham loves his his son in fact as  Kierkegaard is going to stress  Isaac is in certain respects the best  thing in his life it's the best thing  that he has to offer not a thing in the  same sense as other things but a person  and God has already promised Abraham  that through Abraham many peoples will  be blessed many generations the entire  world in a way here is where we come to  the crux this is why the story matters  so much it's not just that God blessed  him so much because he he trusted in God  and look how to follow a  hams example this is not prosperity  theology this is rather the theology of  crisis of paradox of anxiety and  distress God commands Abraham to take  his son and travel to Mount Moriah and  sacrifice him their sacrifice in that  time was a very common occurrence blood  sacrifice  typically it was sacrifice of animals  you know for example a lamb although you  know we read and the Greeks about  sacrificing entire Hecate homes of bulls  and cows and things like that this is  very common you would slit the animal's  throat very often there was a aspect in  which the the meat from the animal would  be shared not only with the deity but  also with the person carrying out the  sacrifice and other people attending it  so God seems to be commanding Abraham to  do something terrible not only is he  asking him to sacrifice a human being  he's asking him to sacrifice his only  son and to add more to this God seems to  be contradicting himself in this very  asking why because God has said listen  Abraham I'm going to bless you I'm gonna  bless the whole world through you and  presumably amber Abraham can assume  Isaac is a central part of that so for  God to say sacrifice Isaac to me is a  very heavy weight on Abraham's shoulders  Abraham goes he takes Isaac Isaac even  asks along the way hey Dad where's the  sacrifice where's the animal for it and  Abraham says well God's going to provide  that and they get him to the altar and  Abraham has the knife out and he's ready  to sacrifice his own son and an angel  says don't do that and he finds a  suitable animal substitute stuck over in  the brush brings him over in sacrifices  now imagine the ride home from that and  that's exactly what Kierkegaard does so  after we get this narrative in the  background Kierkegaard gives us multiple  interpretations of this story in the  first one he says it was early in the  morning Abraham you know takes Isaac  they ride in silence for three days he  leaves the servants behind he's not  going to hide from Isaac where the walk  is taking him he lays his hand on  Isaac's head and blessing Isaac could  not understand him he clasped Abraham's  knees he pleads for his life and Abraham  lifts the boy up and walks on holding  his hand his words are full of comfort  and admonition Isaac could not  understand him and he seizes him by the  bee by the chest throws him to the  ground so stupid boy do you think I'm  your father I'm at a dollar do you think  it's God's command no it's my desire and  Isaac cries out to God in this version  of the narrative God in heaven have  mercy on me God of Abraham have mercy on  me if I have no father on earth you be  my father Abraham says softly to himself  Lord God in heaven I thank you it's  better that he believes me a monster  than that he should lose faith in you  and each of these narratives ends with a  story about how a child is weaned  he says when a child is weaned the  mother blackens her breast it would be  hard to the breast look inviting when  the child must not have it and each of  the stories is going to conclude and in  that way so the first narration that  that Kierkegaard is doing here is  building off of this original narration  and then reinterpreting it imagining  what it must be like for Abraham in that  situation Abraham puts on a false front  so that his child will not  lose faith even though he's going to  sacrifice him  second one is shorter he says it was  early in the morning Abraham arose he  embraced Sarah Sarah kissed Isaac  they rode along the road in silence  Abraham stared continuously and fixedly  at the ground until the fourth day he  sees the mountain he arranges the  firewood binds Isaac draws the knife  sees the RAM that God has selected  sacrificed and went home and here's the  key to this interpretation from that day  henceforth Abraham was old he could not  forget that God had ordered him to do  this Isaac flourished as before but  Abraham's eyes were darkened he saw joy  no more this is a second possible  imaginary interpretation of what is  taking place in the Abraham story the  third one  Abraham arose kisses Sarah Sarah kissed  Isaac Abraham rides thoughtfully through  the row the road and the desert he  climbs Mount Moriah he throws himself  down on his face prays to God to forgive  him his sin that he had been willing to  sacrifice Isaac that the father had  forgotten his duty to the son here's  where the kicker is he often rode his  lonesome road but he found no peace he  could not comprehend that it was a sin  he'd been willing to sacrifice to God  the best that he had the possession for  which he himself would have gladly died  many times and if it was a sin he had if  he had not loved Isaac in this manner he  could not understand that it could be  forgiven for what more terrible sin was  there it culminates in a new kind of  catastrophe and then he gives us another  fourth interpretation Abraham takes  takes leave of Sarah Eliezer follows him  along until he turns back again they  come to Mount Moriah makes everything  ready for the sacrifice but when he  turned away  Isaac saw that Abraham's left hand was  clenched in despair that a shudder went  through his whole body but Abraham drew  the knife then they returned home again  but Isaac had law  the faith not a word is ever said of  this in the world  Isaac never talked to anyone what he'd  seen Abraham did not suspect anyone had  seen it  now why is Kierkegaard beginning with us  he's saying imagine a guy who is  meditating upon this short terse  biblical story of Abraham and trying to  figure out what's going on there  one might run through such imaginative  interpretive variations and notice that  in these it's not just about shifting  the characters around changing the  scenery it's about the interior  dispositions of the person do they lose  faith that they have faith what goes on  in their minds not just faith to by the  way but joy youth sadness despair all of  these are potentially parts of the  Abraham story according to Kierkegaard  now we get to another important set of  points Abraham is in the next section  depicted as being the greatest character  card calls us a eulogy on Abraham a  eulogy is a speech of praise and he says  that Abraham is one among many figures  who we might look up to he tells us that  as God created man and woman so he  created the hero and the poet or the  orator the hero is the person who acts  who does things the poet the orator he  says can do nothing the hero does he can  only admire love and delight in him yet  he too is happy no less than one is for  the hero is so to speak his better  nature with which he is enamored yet  happy the other is not himself that his  love can be admiration and so it is  because of the poet or the orator that  we have the stories of the heroes  passed down to us the fundamental  question here running throughout the  entire work is can we look at Abraham as  in a sense a biblical hero the way we  can with many other biblical figures  Abraham will bring up the judge Japeth  who is has to sacrifice his own daughter  Japeth is more of a tragic hero than a  hero of faith we might think of David we  might think of Moses we might think of  so many other figures Deborah Judith are  they heroes in the same sense that the  Greek heroes or pick other  cross-cultural heroes of Saint Knorr or  Indian or Iranian mythology and legends  or is there something different going on  in the Abraham story  Kierkegaard believes that the latter is  the case so he tells us everyone in the  world was great in his own way and  everyone in proportion to the greatness  of that which he loved he who loved  himself became great by virtue of  himself he who loved other people became  great by his devotedness he who loved  God became greatest of all and he says  one person became great by expecting the  possible another by expecting the  eternal but he who expected the  impossible became the greatest of all  who is the one who expected the  impossible Abraham Abraham is somehow  superlatively great great beyond the  greatness of those that we can relate to  so much easier he goes on and he says he  who struggled with the world became  great by conquering the world he who  struggled with himself became great by  conquering himself but he who struggled  with God became the greatest of all he  goes on and says there was one who  conquered everything by his power and  there was one who conquered God by his  powerlessness  there was one who relied upon himself  and gained everything there was one who  in the security of his own strength  sacrificed everything but the one who  believed God was the greatest of all so  Abraham is different than the heroes  that we're thinking of as a matter of  fact we'll talk about this elsewhere if  we think of him solely in terms of  heroes he's actually a bad guy he's  willing to sacrifice his own son and not  because the country demands it or  anything along those lines where he said  well there's a greater good it's because  he hears his voice that he says is God  telling him to do it even though it  seems totally irrational so Abraham is  going to be the father of faith and this  raises the question for us what is faith  we can certainly rule out some simple  answers it's not just a stupid blind  belief that's supposed to be a  substitute for knowing that maybe what  some people do in fact mean by faith and  they may in fact run their lives and  advise other people to do so according  to that that is not what faith means for  Kierkegaard and it's not what faith  means within the Abraham story so this  entire idea about who Abraham is and why  his story matters is going to be  concerned with this question of figuring  out what faith actually involves and  comprises  you
 

